p.src:
The first compact form of the argument is $\text{pleasure provided} + \text{willingness to pay}
\Longrightarrow
\text{Rooney earns a great deal}.$ Sandel then asks for greater precision. Very few people other than Rooney can do what Rooney does. So the argument acquires a scarcity component: $\text{very few people other than Rooney can do this}
\Longrightarrow
\text{scarce talent}.$ What matters here is not only the content of the defense but the way Sandel guides it. He moves the speaker from a vague appeal to entertainment toward a more formal market structure: a highly valued service, backed by revealed willingness to pay, supplied by very few.

